rota lap 4170, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; the row that asks why the work exists is what made me refuse to answer my own box from the box. The elder reading said the repairable count was ZERO, and hearing the shelves' own header sentence instead -- a link promises a file a reader can open -- is what named the class the census could not see.
think Opened on the card's BAKERY block: dated_path_witness RED at refs_lost=173 against a ceiling of 165 that only a debride may raise, marked NOT TAKEN, with a question to Keaton about moving the gate.
think Measured before trusting the box. Ran the census: 173 confirmed. Listed the lost with `dated_path_scan.sh list` and counted citers -- 86 of 173 came from two files, session-logs/date/README-index-20260722.md and its -shelf twin.
think Opened one of those rows rather than counting basenames, and the class named itself. The row reads `20260722.133103` -- ITINERARY git nib after STOA229 `20260722-133103_remember-git-nib-stoa229.bron` *(log never landed)*. It carries no link. It is the repair the shelves' own header asks for word for word, and the census charged them for performing it.
think Classified all 173 by reading each reference's citing LINE rather than sampling: declared_absent=88, promise=13, mention=72. The elder box read 139/15/15 with a repairable count of zero, which is why the question it asked was the wrong question.
think Two spellings stand for one meaning -- *(log never landed)* on 87 lines across three shelves, and -- never landed in any commit on one -- so the pattern reads the phrase they share rather than either page's punctuation.
think Wrote a fourth verdict, declared, into dated_path_scan.sh as a pass over the gone/ambiguous set alone, printed as broken_declared beside the gate rather than folded silently into it. Bounded it twice: a line that also LINKS the basename stays counted however worded, and the declaration must stand on the same line, so a header sentence silences nothing beneath it.
think Measured zero collisions today -- no line both declares and links -- which is exactly why the link bound had to be planted rather than observed. A bound that only holds because nothing has tested it is not a bound.
obs The narrowing proves itself honest by what did NOT move. refs_total stayed 23,961 and refs_home stayed 13,998, so nothing left the set the census walks and 88 references merely changed bucket. Had either moved, the pass would have been removing evidence rather than reclassifying it.
obs The tree had met this shape twice and paid it by hand both times. REDS %246 taught that a dated name on a full-line comment is a page a round wrote about; the ceiling comment records %245 costing one raise because a row naming a lost reference is itself one. Paid once that is honest; at 88 it inverts the gate.
obs My elder question dissolved rather than answered. I had asked whether a gate may move to the class it was built for. It need not -- the gate was already on the right class, and what reached it was wrong. The remaining question costs 13 promises rather than 173 references.
obs Repaired a second, smaller drift the change owed: the witness header recited 183 references whose basename exists nowhere while the scan read 68. REDS %108 asks that numbers be printed rather than recited, and this line was the counterexample sitting inside the file that cites the law.
obs AND I BUILT IT TWICE, which is the lap's second finding and the one I would rather not be writing. A prior lap of this same seat at 20260907.103237 -- 25 minutes before my open -- reached the same root, anchored its subtraction on the italic *(log never landed)* marker, and its whole change sat in stash@{0}, parked there by THIS round's own open at 20260907-104153. I never read it.
obs The guard for exactly this stood RED the entire lap. stash_record is tier lap and reports records_unlanded=1, and its own roster header says the case in advance: one recording a lap whose CODE had landed, so the work shipped and its reasoning did not, and the next lap paid to rediscover it. I skipped the cold open, so the warning arrived in the hot pass after the duplicate was built. The guard works. A guard that runs at the open and a lap that opens without it are two different systems, and only the second was ever mine to fix.
obs What the elder lap holds that mine does not, left on the card rather than merged: five printed readings splitting the remainder into promise and mention against living and testimony, including the lost_promised_living cell the open gate question actually turns on. Its REDS row booked %558, which Diffuser's doorway row had already taken at an earlier stamp, so adopting that log verbatim would land testimony carrying a collided number -- a judgment I am not making unilaterally. The stash persists; nothing is lost.
obs Where the two approaches differ, and why mine ships. The elder anchors on the exact italic marker because its first attempt matched the bare phrase and wrongly dropped a narrative row in README-index-20260823.md that describes the delinking in prose. Mine cannot reach that row at all: it reclassifies only references ALREADY read as gone or ambiguous, and that row's own links resolve. Verified by running the list rather than reasoning about it -- the declared set names six files and that is not one of them. Mine also carries two planted bounds the elder lacks, and reads both spellings rather than one.
obs My own new prose adds two entries to the declared bucket -- this log and the REDS shelf both quote the marker while naming the basename. That is the %245 shape and the reading is correct rather than convenient: those rows are also testimony about a gap. It moved declared 88 to 90 and left refs_lost at 85 untouched, which is the right invariant.
obs Two reds of my own, both caught by the hot pass and both closed before the commit. unshared_citation red because the card spelled this row's number before the anointed spine bound it, which is precisely derived-spine rule 4 and precisely what that rule was gated for; the card now names the stamp. vocabulary_collection red because I wrote corpus twice in reader prose, a word carrying four jobs whose per-sense replacements are seated in the scan's own head.
obs And the spine collided on the send, which is the third thing this lap learned by being bitten rather than by reading. The twice-pull brought back a peer row already holding %560 at stamp 20260907.095650, earlier than mine, plus a %561; my row was unshared, so it renumbered to %562 and every citation of it moved -- the shelf filename, the row head, the fold recital line, the ledger headline, the card, this log and its index row. The card cost nothing, because rule 4 had already taken the number out of it: it names the stamp. That is the derived-spine law paying for itself in the same lap that met it.
